<div class="article-body">
<h2 id="what-it-is">What the 70% rule is</h2>
<p>The 70% rule is a screening formula that fix-and-flip investors use to cap their offer on a property that needs work, and that wholesalers use to work backwards to the most they can offer a seller. It is a rule of thumb, not a valuation. It does not say what a property is worth; it says what an investor with a typical cost structure can afford to pay for it.</p>
<p>Its value is speed. Two inputs, the after repair value and the repair estimate, produce a first answer in seconds. That is what you need when you are screening many leads and only a few of them deserve a full analysis with financing, taxes and timelines worked out line by line.</p>
<p>Wholesalers run the same math from the other side. Your end buyer, usually a flipper, will pay no more than their own maximum. Your fee has to fit underneath that ceiling, so your maximum offer to the seller is the buyer's maximum minus your fee. Offer above it and either your fee or the buyer's margin comes out of the deal, and experienced buyers simply walk away.</p>
<h2 id="formula">The formula</h2>
<details class="article-formula"><summary>See the 70% rule formulas</summary><p>The short form most investors quote: maximum allowable offer = (ARV × 70%) − repair costs.</p><p>The fuller form, and the one our calculator uses: maximum offer = (ARV × 70%) − repairs − closing and holding costs − your wholesale fee. The first three terms give the end buyer's price. Your fee comes out of that price, never on top of it.</p></details>
<p>Three terms do the work. The <strong>after repair value (ARV)</strong> is what the property should sell for once renovated, estimated from recent sales of similar renovated homes nearby. <strong>Repairs</strong> are the buyer's full rehab budget, not a cosmetic touch-up. <strong>Closing and holding costs</strong> are the transfer, title, tax, insurance and financing costs the buyer's price has to absorb while the project runs.</p>
<p>The 70% itself is a convention, not a constant. It is the share of the finished value that the offer, the repairs and the other costs are allowed to consume between them. The remaining 30% is the buyer's allowance for everything the formula leaves out, plus their profit.</p>
<h2 id="example">Worked example</h2>
<p>This example uses the values already filled in on the Wholesale Calculator, so you can check every figure there.</p>
<div class="article-example"><span class="example-label">Worked example</span><h3>A $250,000 ARV with $30,000 of repairs</h3><span class="example-label">Inputs</span><p>$250,000 after repair value, $30,000 estimated repairs, a 70% investor discount, $5,000 of closing and holding costs, and a $10,000 wholesale fee. The seller is asking $125,000.</p><span class="example-label">Calculation</span><ol><li><span>70% of ARV: $250,000 × 0.70</span><strong>$175,000.00</strong></li><li><span>Less repairs: $175,000 − $30,000</span><strong>$145,000.00</strong></li><li><span>End buyer's price, less closing and holding: $145,000 − $5,000</span><strong>$140,000.00</strong></li><li class="example-result"><span class="example-label">Result</span><span>Maximum offer, less your fee: $140,000 − $10,000</span><strong data-example="70-percent-rule">$130,000.00</strong></li><li><span>Offer as a share of ARV: $130,000 ÷ $250,000</span><strong>52%</strong></li><li><span>Headroom at the $125,000 asking price: $130,000 − $125,000</span><strong>$5,000.00</strong></li></ol><span class="example-label">Explanation</span><p class="example-caption">The deal works at the asking price, with $5,000 to spare. If the seller wanted $150,000 there would be no wholesale deal at these numbers: the price alone is more than the buyer can pay once repairs are counted, before any fee. The $5,000 of headroom is also the only cushion if the repair estimate creeps up, which is why the inputs below matter more than the arithmetic.</p></div>
<h2 id="covers">What the 30% covers</h2>
<p>The 30% held back is not profit handed to the buyer for nothing. It is a single allowance that has to pay for several real costs at once:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Selling costs.</strong> Agent commissions and closing costs when the buyer resells the finished property.</li><li><strong>Buying costs.</strong> The closing costs on the purchase itself: title, transfer and lender fees.</li><li><strong>Holding costs.</strong> Loan interest, property taxes, insurance and utilities while the renovation runs.</li><li><strong>The buyer's profit.</strong> The return that makes the project worth its risk and effort.</li><li><strong>A cushion for surprises.</strong> Repairs that grow, a slower sale, or a final price below the ARV.</li></ul>
<p>Bundling all of that into one percentage is the rule's whole design, and also its weakness. Real costs do not scale neatly with the price: closing costs on a $120,000 house are not half those on a $240,000 house, and a nine-month renovation spends far more of the allowance on holding costs than a three-month one. That is why the percentage gets adjusted.</p>
<h2 id="adjusting">When to adjust the percentage</h2>
<p>Seventy percent is a starting point. Buyers loosen it when the dollars are large and the work is light, and tighten it when the risk sits in the rehab or the resale.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Typical discount</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Competitive market, light repairs, higher ARV</td><td>75 to 80%</td><td>Fixed costs are a smaller share of a large value, and buyers compete harder for clean deals.</td></tr><tr><td>A standard single-family deal</td><td>70%</td><td>The classic starting point: typical costs, typical risk.</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy rehab or a slow market</td><td>60 to 65%</td><td>Bigger repair risk and longer holding times eat into the cushion the 30% was meant to protect.</td></tr><tr><td>A very low-priced property</td><td>65% or less</td><td>Fixed costs such as closing and holding take a larger share of a small ARV, so 70% leaves too little room.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Your buyers set the real number. If the flippers you sell to screen deals at 75%, your offers can follow them; if they hold at 65%, a 70% offer will not move. Our Wholesale Calculator shows an offer ladder at four discounts on the same property, so you can see how the maximum moves before you negotiate.</p>
<h2 id="inputs">Getting ARV and repairs right</h2>
<p>The formula is exact. Its two main inputs are estimates, and the answer is only as good as they are.</p>
<ul><li><strong>ARV.</strong> Base it on recently sold, renovated properties nearby that are similar in size, age and finish. A listing price is not an ARV, and neither is the seller's estimate. An ARV that is $20,000 too high inflates the maximum offer by $14,000 at a 70% discount, before any other mistake is made.</li><li><strong>Repairs.</strong> Price the buyer's full rehab, not the cosmetic version of it. A contractor walkthrough beats a guess, and a contingency belongs in the number, because every extra repair dollar comes straight off the maximum offer.</li><li><strong>Your fee.</strong> Decide it before you negotiate and keep it inside the formula. Fees of $5,000 to $15,000 are common on single-family deals, but a fee can only ever be as large as the spread the numbers leave between the seller's price and the buyer's ceiling.</li></ul>
<h2 id="limitations">Limitations of the rule</h2>
<ul><li><strong>It is a screen, not an analysis.</strong> It ignores your buyer's financing terms, their actual tax and insurance costs, and how long the project really takes. A long renovation can spend most of the 30% on holding costs alone.</li><li><strong>Both inputs are estimates.</strong> The ARV depends on comparable sales holding up; the repair figure depends on what is found once work starts. Treat the result as a ceiling to test, not a promise.</li><li><strong>It says nothing about legality.</strong> Assigning a purchase contract is legal in most of the US, but some states and cities require a real estate licence, or written disclosure, to market a contract you do not own. Check your local rules before your first deal.</li></ul>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Adding your fee on top of the buyer's price.</strong> The fee comes out of your offer to the seller. Add it on top instead and you have quietly handed your buyer a worse deal than the rule allows, and it will sit.</li><li><strong>Using the asking price or current value as the ARV.</strong> The formula runs on the renovated value. Run it on today's value and the maximum comes out far too low; run it on an optimistic renovated value and it comes out too high.</li><li><strong>Forgetting closing and holding costs.</strong> Leaving them out is how a 70% deal quietly becomes a 75% deal, with the difference taken from the buyer's margin.</li><li><strong>Treating 70% as a law.</strong> The right percentage moves with the market, the price band and the rehab. A single fixed number misprices deals at both ends.</li><li><strong>Underestimating repairs to make a deal fit.</strong> Your buyer will price the repairs again, less kindly. A deal that only works with a low repair estimate does not work.</li><li><strong>Offering your maximum as your opening bid.</strong> The maximum is a ceiling, not an opener. Leave room to negotiate, or the first counter-offer ends the deal.</li></ul>
<h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
<ul><li>The 70% rule: offer no more than 70% of the after repair value, minus repairs. Wholesalers also subtract closing and holding costs and their own fee.</li><li>In the worked example, a $250,000 ARV with $30,000 of repairs gives an end buyer price of $140,000 and a maximum offer of $130,000 after a $10,000 fee.</li><li>The 30% held back pays for buying, holding and selling costs and the buyer's profit. It is a cushion, not a gift.</li><li>Adjust the percentage to the deal: 75 to 80% in competitive markets with light work, 60 to 65% on heavy rehabs and slow sales.</li><li>The rule is only as good as its ARV and repair estimate. Both deserve more care than the arithmetic.</li><li>Your maximum is a ceiling. Offer above it and the deal stops working for you, for your buyer, or for both.</li></ul>
<h2 id="faqs">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What is the 70% rule in real estate?</h3>
<p>It is a rule of thumb that caps an investor's offer at 70% of a property's after repair value, minus the cost of repairs. The result is called the maximum allowable offer (MAO). The 30% held back is meant to cover buying, holding and selling costs and leave the buyer a profit.</p>
<h3>How do wholesalers use the 70% rule?</h3>
<p>A wholesaler works backwards from the end buyer. The buyer's maximum is 70% of ARV minus repairs and closing costs; the wholesaler subtracts their assignment fee from that figure, and the result is the most they can offer the seller. In the worked example: a $140,000 buyer price minus a $10,000 fee gives a $130,000 maximum offer.</p>
<h3>What is a maximum allowable offer (MAO)?</h3>
<p>It is the highest price you can pay, or put under contract, while still leaving your end buyer their margin and keeping your own fee. It is the output of the 70% rule formula, and it falls as repairs, closing costs or your fee rise.</p>
<h3>Is the 70% rule always 70%?</h3>
<p>No. It is a convention that investors adjust, commonly between 60% and 80%. Competitive markets and higher-value properties support a higher percentage; heavy rehabs, slow markets and very cheap properties call for a lower one. Your buyers' criteria matter more than the textbook number.</p>
<h3>Does the 70% rule work for rental properties?</h3>
<p>It was built for fix-and-flip and wholesale deals. A landlord can use it as a first screen on a property that needs renovation, but a rental decision turns on rent and yield over years, so follow it with a full rental analysis, for example with our <a href="../../tools/property-investment-calculator/">Property Investment Calculator</a>.</p>
<h3>What if the seller will not accept my maximum offer?</h3>
<p>Then at those numbers there is no wholesale deal. The honest options are a smaller fee, a buyer who accepts a thinner discount, or walking away. Offering above your maximum turns your fee into a loss the moment the buyer re-prices the repairs.</p>
